From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:42:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] PROTOTYPE pvr: cache compiled graphics pipelines

Local experiment for amazon-suez, never to be sent (Imagination declines
LLM-generated code in src/imagination).

pvr took a VkPipelineCache and never used it, so every graphics pipeline
was compiled from SPIR-V in every process: about 0.7 s of a GTK4 app's
first frame on GX6250 (zink's pipelines for GSK's GL renderer, measured
with perf). Cache the compiled result of a whole graphics pipeline (both
stages' pco_data and binding tables, binaries, the fragment shader's
varying interpolation) as a raw-data object, which the runtime also puts
in the disk cache. A device-level weak cache covers callers that pass no
VkPipelineCache.

The key hashes the stages (vk_pipeline_hash_shader_stage), the layout's
set-layout hashes and push ranges, the graphics state by value (all_state
is now zeroed before vk_graphics_pipeline_state_fill() so padding is
deterministic; the sample-locations pointer is hashed by value), the
enabled features, and PCO_DEBUG, PVR_DEBUG and SUEZ_PVR_2D_VIEW_OF_3D.
Render-pass pipelines, capture flags and transform feedback are not
cached. SUEZ_PVR_NO_PIPELINE_CACHE disables it; SUEZ_PVR_CACHE_DEBUG
prints hits and misses.

Measured on the board: gnome-calculator on GSK's GL renderer, 7 pipelines
per launch, all hit from the second launch with an unchanged Mesa GL
shader cache; gles-layers check and probes still pass.

Generated-by: LLM
